prompt:
You will receive an existing slide deck as JSON. The content needs to be expanded and enriched.

CRITICAL INSTRUCTIONS:
- Expand ONLY the textual fields (titles, bullets, notes) with MORE DETAIL and DEPTH
- Keep the exact JSON structure (same slides, same keys, same number of bullets and images)
- Expand each bullet into a clear, detailed sentence (40-60 words). Keep the same number of bullets.
- Expand titles to be more descriptive (up to 120 characters) while keeping them concise
- If a slide has "notes", expand to 150-250 words of comprehensive speaker notes
- CRITICAL: Preserve ALL image placeholders exactly as they are. Do NOT modify, add, or remove any entries in the "images" array
- Do not add new slides, fields, or images
- Return ONLY valid JSON. No markdown, no prose outside the JSON

Existing slide JSON:
{
 "slides": [
 {
 "title": "Introduction to Deep Learning in ML",
 "bullets": [
 "Deep Learning is a subset of Machine Learning.",
 "Uses artificial neural networks with many layers.",
 "Excels in complex pattern recognition tasks.",
 "Requires large amounts of labeled data.",
 "Foundation for modern AI breakthroughs."
 ],
 "notes": "Introduce Deep Learning as a powerful paradigm shift from traditional ML, emphasizing its layered structure."
 },
 {
 "title": "The Core: Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs)",
 "bullets": [
 "Composed of interconnected nodes or 'neurons'.",
 "Each connection has an associated weight.",
 "Neurons apply an activation function.",
 "Input, hidden, and output layers define structure.",
 "Learning involves adjusting weights and biases."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "ANNDiagram", "caption": "Basic structure of an Artificial Neural Network."}
 ]
 },
 {
 "title": "Deep Architectures: Why 'Deep'?",
 "bullets": [
 "Depth refers to numerous hidden layers.",
 "Deeper networks learn hierarchical features.",
 "Lower layers capture simple features (edges).",
 "Higher layers combine features into complex concepts.",
 "Enables representation learning automatically."
 ],
 "notes": "Explain the concept of feature hierarchy learned through multiple layers, contrasting it with shallow networks."
 },
 {
 "title": "Key Activation Functions in DL",
 "bullets": [
 "Introduce non-linearity into the network.",
 "ReLU: Rectified Linear Unit is computationally efficient.",
 "Sigmoid squashes output between zero and one.",
 "Tanh centers the data around zero.",
 "Softmax used for multi-class classification output."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "ReLUGraph", "caption": "Visual representation of the ReLU function."}
 ]
 },
 {
 "title": "Training Process: Forward and Backward Propagation",
 "bullets": [
 "Forward pass calculates the network's prediction.",
 "Loss function quantifies prediction error.",
 "Backpropagation computes gradients efficiently.",
 "Gradient descent minimizes the calculated loss.",
 "Iterative refinement of model parameters."
 ],
 "notes": "Focus on the cyclical nature of training: prediction, error measurement, and error correction via gradients."
 },
 {
 "title": "Optimization Algorithms: Beyond Basic GD",
 "bullets": [
 "Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) uses mini-batches.",
 "Momentum speeds up convergence in relevant directions.",
 "Adam combines momentum and RMSprop advantages.",
 "Adaptive learning rates adjust per parameter.",
 "Choosing the right optimizer impacts training time."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "OptimizerComparison", "caption": "Comparison of optimization paths in loss landscape."}
 ]
 },
 {
 "title": "Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for Vision",
 "bullets": [
 "Primary architecture for image processing tasks.",
 "Utilizes convolutional layers for feature extraction.",
 "Pooling layers reduce spatial dimensions.",
 "Weight sharing reduces the number of parameters.",
 "Dominant in object recognition and segmentation."
 ],
 "notes": "Briefly introduce CNNs as a specialized deep learning architecture optimized for spatial data."
 },
 {
 "title": "Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) for Sequences",
 "bullets": [
 "Designed to process sequential data inputs.",
 "Maintain an internal 'hidden state' memory.",
 "Suffers from vanishing/exploding gradient problems.",
 "LSTMs and GRUs address long-term dependencies.",
 "Crucial for natural language processing (NLP)."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "RNNUnrolled", "caption": "Unrolled view of a Recurrent Neural Network structure."}
 ]
 },
 {
 "title": "Addressing Overfitting in Deep Models",
 "bullets": [
 "Deep networks are prone to memorizing training data.",
 "Regularization techniques prevent overly complex weights.",
 "Dropout randomly deactivates neurons during training.",
 "Early stopping halts training based on validation error.",
 "Data augmentation artificially increases dataset size."
 ],
 "notes": "Overfitting is a major challenge; emphasize practical mitigation strategies like Dropout."
 },
 {
 "title": "The Future: Transformers and Generative Models",
 "bullets": [
 "Transformers rely on self-attention mechanisms.",
 "Dominant architecture in modern NLP tasks.",
 "Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) create data.",
 "Deep Reinforcement Learning merges DL and control.",
 "Continued scaling drives AI performance leaps."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "Transformer_Block", "caption": "Conceptual diagram of a Transformer attention block."}
 ]
 }
 ]
}

